\begin{abstract}
In over-the-counter corporate bond markets, dealers compete for client trades by
quoting bid and ask prices. Tighter quotes attract more business, but they also
attract informed customers who are more likely to trade ahead of adverse price
moves, leaving the dealer holding the risk. As dealers increasingly use machine
learning to set quotes, they retrain these models on the trades their own quotes
attract, creating a feedback loop in which each model reshapes the market that
generates its next training data. The central question is therefore not only
whether a quoting model performs well, but whether the market it creates remains
stable as the model learns from it. Existing performative prediction theory gives
a sharp stability condition, yet expresses it through abstract properties of the
learning objective that a trading desk cannot measure before deployment. We
introduce \textsc{Reflex}, a framework that replaces those unobservable
quantities with three measurable features of dealer behavior: how strongly
trading volume responds to tighter quotes, how sharply the dealer's objective
bends around its optimum, and how quickly informed flow increases as spreads
narrow. \textsc{Reflex} combines these into a single retraining modulus,
a pre-deployment stability margin that is estimated from a desk's own quote and
execution history and that predicts whether repeated retraining will converge or
amplify itself. In simulation, predicted and measured stability agree within
$8\%$, while adding competing dealers increases instability by $1.74\times$ with
two dealers and $3.16\times$ with three, as predicted. Where ordinary retraining
becomes unstable at a modulus of $1.21$, a structurally anchored correction
converges even as blind retraining collapses. Calibrated over $36$ years of
public market data, the available stability headroom falls roughly $4.4\times$
for investment grade and $4.3\times$ for high yield between calm and crisis
regimes. \textsc{Reflex} thus turns an abstract convergence theorem into
a market-level safety margin that can be evaluated before an automated quoting
system goes live.
\end{abstract}
